## Retrospective

The [Apache CMS](https://web.archive.org/web/20120112045033/http://www.apache.org/dev/cms) was always ahead of its time.  Never more clearly evidenced than in its content dependency management functionality in the early years of the Sun donation of OpenOffice to Apache.

To be clear: when others speak of dependency management, they are predominately concerned with software dependencies, not [content dependencies](dependencies).

It all boils down to well-regulated content "includes" in the templating+build system, which is not at all the same thing as software deps.

This feature was absolutely critical in supporting Apache's massive <https://OpenOffice.org> website.  The RDBMS CMS Sun provided would fall over and die if you wanted to fix a typo.

If you look at the content editing going on with OpenOffice's website on GitHub lately, you can clearly see a massive dropoff in activity when Apache forced them off the Apache CMS, and channeled all contribution activity through GitHub PR.

Without some gluing CMS like service that can get a contributor into an editing session for the page they want to repair IN A SINGLE CLICK, the cognitive energy is too great to go fish that page out of a github repo, fork the repo, edit the page, commit the change, push it, and create a PR, wait until someone merges the PR, and then [wait 10-15 minutes for the staging build to finish](https://ci-builds.apache.org/job/OpenOffice/job/OpenOffice-org-Staging-Site-Build/),have a committer find and review the changed content somewhere on the [staging site](https://www.staging.openoffice.org/), and then wait for someone to promote the build to production , and then [wait another 5-10 minutes for the publication build to finish](https://ci-builds.apache.org/job/OpenOffice/job/OpenOffice-org-Publish-Site/), and then wait for gitpubsub to push the new content out to Apache's edge webservers.

With the Apache CMS (webgui), sharing a committable "patch/diff" over email was a one-click operation for any person on Earth, as well as a one-click operation to commit+build+publish for a committer to apply on the project. The whole thing revolved around the sharing of capability URLs. They allowed an Apache committer on the project to "clone" the zfs filesystem of a contributor's (uncommitted) checkout; and subsequently inspect, alter, and commit that cloned checkout by the Apache committer themselves **as the committer and not the contributor**.  Once that commit happens, the CMS not only built it in *seconds*, but it also provided **links** to the live rendering of the content on the staging site for review prior to promotion to production.

The whole One-Click Amazon Patent was critical to customer satisfaction for them.  Same here; but The Apache CMS was all alone in this space.

The Apache CMS (webgui) was that essential coordination switchboard between all that volunteer energy that has sadly left the org behind.

There are several excuses Apache's Infrastructure Leadership made as to why it was dropped:

1. a bus-factor of 0 post my 2018 resignation

2. perl, not python

3. buggy (unreliable zfs clones from a tiny FreeBSD jail)

4. ugly (gee thanks, Rich!)

But the real motive was spite.  By the time it was finally decommissioned, it had been running on autopilot in a FreeBSD jail on Brutus for over 5 years. The only maintenance required was (quarterly?) host reboots due to item 3 above.

When push came to shove, I offered to host the OpenOffice website on Orion at a steep discount. The Apache Infrastructure Team put Dave Fisher in the unenviable position of declaring his now exclusive loyalties:  to me and the project, or to "The ASF".

Dave was the key innovator and collaborator behind the scalability successes of The Apache CMS's incremental build technology.  He didn't solve the problems; but he worked productively with me on how I added the scalability features he needed to ensure high performance builds from The Apache CMS as applied to the OpenOffice website- which at the time was delivering north of 25M requests a day! Together we discovered a profound application of SSI in his efforts, which I believe are still carried through in the JBake templating system in use today.


